WEBVTT

00:00:00.132 --> 00:00:03.967
INSERT INTO followed by SELECT is a useful data-preparation pattern.

00:00:03.967 --> 00:00:10.642
Instead of typing individual VALUES, the SELECT query produces the rows that should be inserted into another

00:00:10.642 --> 00:00:10.942
table.

00:00:10.942 --> 00:00:15.887
This appears frequently in staging, ETL and ELT workflows, archives, reporting tables, and derived datasets.

00:00:15.887 --> 00:00:20.407
Run the example and inspect the newly created table containing patients in the requested age group.

00:00:20.407 --> 00:00:26.827
The important idea is that the SELECT controls which rows and columns move into the destination.

00:00:26.827 --> 00:00:30.402
Before using this pattern on larger data, verify the SELECT by itself first.

00:00:30.402 --> 00:00:33.612
Make sure it returns exactly the records you intend to insert.

00:00:33.612 --> 00:00:37.122
That follows the same safety principle we have used throughout the module:

00:00:37.122 --> 00:00:41.017
preview the result before performing the write operation.

00:00:41.017 --> 00:00:45.142
Think of SELECT work as progressively shaping a question.

00:00:45.142 --> 00:00:48.937
First identify the rows and columns you need, then filter, derive expressions, sort, and limit for inspection.

00:00:48.937 --> 00:00:52.802
A useful habit is to test each part on a small result before adding more complexity,

00:00:52.802 --> 00:00:57.022
especially when a filter contains NULL-sensitive logic.
